  "ai": "The record: 18 wins, 8 losses and 3 scratches over 29 trades, up 7.2% per trade on average. Since Oct 14 (the V3 trades plus the KWEB put) it is 7–3 with no scratches, up 6.1% per trade, with winners averaging +17.3% and losers −20.1%.\n\nWhat's working: the spread and fill problems from Oct 9 to 13 (wide spreads, paying over the ask, thin volume) haven't come up in a single trade since, and the scratches stopped with them. Losers are still cut on a plan: the SOFI 14.50 call when the fade kept going, the MARA 8 put at its stop, the SOFI 15.50 call on a thesis exit. Scaling out on the F 12.50 call locked in the gain, and buying the SOFI 14.50 call back a day after cutting it turned a −17% into a +20% on the same contract.\n\nWhat needs work: losses are now bigger than wins. Since Oct 14 the three losers averaged −20.1% against +17.3% for the seven winners, so the edge rests on winning about seven trades in ten, and a stretch of 50/50 days would turn it negative. The MARA 8 put lost 26% on a stop set at the stock's morning high, which is a wide stop on a contract that cheap; a closer level, or a stop on the option's own price, would cap that. Meanwhile the two smallest wins, the RIVN 13 call (+5%) and the PFE 30 call (+10%), were sold for less than half the size of a typical loss. Letting winners run closer to the size of the stops, or tightening the stops toward the size of the wins, is the fix. Entry discipline is the other leak: the SOFI 15.50 call was bought before its breakout printed, and the RIVN 13 call came after the entry window had closed.\n\nThe three newest trades used contracts three to four weeks from expiry instead of weeklies, and all three won, including the KWEB put held overnight and the BAC 51 put flipped the same day for +20%. That suits any trade that might be held, since a longer-dated contract loses less to time decay while it sits, but three trades are too few to judge. V3 is 6–3 after 9 trades (+4.3% per trade) against V2's +8.6% over 20; about 20 V3 trades should settle which is better.",
